{
  "choice": "within",
  "known_facts": {
    "text": "On 24 May 2023, NVIDIA guided to Q2 FY2024 revenue of USD 11.00 billion, plus or minus 2%. It reported Q1 revenue of USD 7.19 billion, up 19% sequentially, so the outlook called for a substantial step-up.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "passage_id": "P1",
        "quote": "Revenue is expected to be $11.00 billion, plus or minus 2%."
      },
      {
        "passage_id": "P4",
        "quote": "Revenue was $7.19 billion, down 13% from a year ago and up 19% sequentially."
      }
    ]
  },
  "base_case": {
    "text": "As of 25 May 2023, I expect Q2 FY2024 total revenue to finish within guidance. Strong cloud and internet-company demand was already lifting Data Center revenue, and I expect the announced supply ramp to turn enough of that demand into shipments and recognized revenue in Q2. The outlook already appears to account for the ramp, so strong demand alone is not a reason to call a beat.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "passage_id": "P4",
        "quote": "The revenue growth reflects strong demand from large consumer internet companies and cloud service providers."
      },
      {
        "passage_id": "P2",
        "quote": "We are significantly increasing our supply to meet surging demand for them"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternative": {
    "text": "An above-range result is plausible if NVIDIA ships more Hopper GPUs than its outlook assumes and recognizes the additional revenue in Q2. Management reported rising supply and surging demand, but did not establish how much would ship by quarter-end.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "passage_id": "P2",
        "quote": "We are significantly increasing our supply to meet surging demand for them"
      },
      {
        "passage_id": "P4",
        "quote": "led by growing demand for generative AI and large language models using GPUs based on our NVIDIA Hopper and Ampere architectures."
      }
    ]
  },
  "revision_signal": {
    "text": "Before earnings, I would move toward an above-range forecast if major cloud providers confirmed broad H100 instance availability ahead of their announced schedules, backed by more GPUs delivered by quarter-end than planned. An earlier launch alone would not establish higher NVIDIA shipments.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "passage_id": "P2",
        "quote": "Our entire data center family of products — H100, Grace CPU, Grace Hopper Superchip, NVLink, Quantum 400 InfiniBand and BlueField-3 DPU — is in production."
      },
      {
        "passage_id": "P4",
        "quote": "The revenue growth reflects strong demand from large consumer internet companies and cloud service providers."
      }
    ]
  }
}

